Quick summary
- The excerpt follows Monseigneur Bienvenu (M. Alton), a deeply charitable bishop who lives in voluntary poverty and tends the poor; his life and pastoral work are sketched in detail. The narrative then follows several other strands: the convict Humphrey Waylon (his suffering in the galleys and his moral crisis), Miracle (a young woman who falls into destitution and prostitution), and the rise of Father/Monsieur Madeleine (a benefactor/manufacturer). The text alternates moral reflection, social critique (penal system, poverty), and intimate portraits of people confronting suffering, mercy, and social judgment.

Key characters (short)
