- Mademoiselle Baptistine & Madame Magloire — his sister and housekeeper; models of humble devotion.  
- Humphrey Waylon — a peasant condemned for theft, nineteen years in the galleys; psychological descent into hatred and a tortured moral reawakening.  
- Miracle — young woman who becomes destitute after abandonment; tragic arc into survival and degradation.  
- Tholomyes, Listolier, Fameuil, Blachevelle — young students and their lovers (a background of flirtation and betrayal).  
- Thénardier (and Madame Thénardier) — petty innkeepers who exploit an abandoned child (early hints of the later Thénardier figure).  
- Father/Monsieur Madeleine — industrialist/philanthropist who transforms a town and embodies civic charity.  
- Inspector Noreen — a rigid police figure; contrast to Madeleine/Bienvenu.

Major themes & motifs (high level)
- Charity vs. judgement: repeated contrasts between mercy (Bienvenu, Madeleine) and social condemnation (town gossip, police).  
- Social causes of crime: prison/penal system as a maker of hardened criminals; starvation and punishment cycle.  
- Redemption and conscience: characters face opportunities for repentance (Waylon’s crisis; Miracle’s encounter with Madeleine).  
- Hypocrisy and public opinion: small-town rumor, moralizing women, and double standards (men’s libertinism vs. women’s ruin).  
- Imagery: domestic objects (silverware, beds), gardens, night/sky, scaffold/guillotine — used as moral symbols.

Contextual note
